---
description: >
  QA log tracking five rounds of TUI implementation drift — keyboard handling, deep/shallow toggle, cursor-filter desync, frozen tree cursor during drill-in, and plan parser format mismatch.
intent: >
  Realign the TUI implementation with the .aide spec — specifically the
  drill-in keyboard interaction, the deep/shallow toggle correctness, the
  cursor-filter desync, and the untested drill-in Enter path.
  (Round 2: one regression found in the fix for issue #4.)
  (Round 3: hint text inconsistency during search + dir cursor, and missing test coverage for that path.)
  (Round 4: tree cursor frozen during drill-in mode — spec says tree remains navigable throughout.)
  (Round 5: plan parser format mismatch and aide_discover description parity gap.)
misalignment:
  - implementation-drift
  - test-gap
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues — Round 1 (all resolved in fix loop)

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.tsx:141-185` — Drill-in mode keyboard handler handles only Escape/Backspace. Up/Down (scroll) and Enter (expand section) are never dispatched to DrillInPanel. The footer hint "[↑↓] scroll  [enter] expand section" is shown but the actions it promises are dead — no scroll position state exists in App, and DrillInPanel's `expanded` Set is never mutated because no keypress ever reaches it.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "Body sections rendered as collapsible or scrollable content below the frontmatter card" (the "enter to expand section" behavior is spec-mandated interactivity, not cosmetic)
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.tsx:121-138` — Toggling deep view OFF (line 122-124) only sets `isDeepView = false`; it does NOT restore the shallow `treeNodes`/`flatNodes`. Deep summaries therefore persist in the tree after the user toggles back to shallow view.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "toggle between shallow (locations + types only) and deep (with summaries) views" — the toggle must be reversible; summaries must disappear when the user returns to shallow.
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/TreePanel/index.tsx:26,81` — The `isDeepView` prop is declared and received but never read inside the render body. Summary display (line 54, 68-72) is gated only on `file.summary` being truthy, not on `isDeepView`. If the shallow scan ever returns non-empty summaries (or after a deep scan populates them and the above bug leaves them in state), shallow view will still show summaries. The prop is structural dead code.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "shallow view shows only file locations and type tags" — summaries must be conditionally suppressed by the `isDeepView` flag.
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.tsx:59-67` vs `src/cli/TreePanel/index.tsx:82-95` — App and TreePanel each compute their own independent `visibleNodes` from the same `flatNodes` + `searchFilter` inputs, but with different dir-inclusion logic. App always includes all dir nodes (line 61: `if kind === "dir" return true`), while TreePanel only includes a dir if at least one child file matches (lines 84-89). This means the cursor index App calculates is over a larger set than what TreePanel renders, causing the highlighted cursor row to be wrong (or invisible) during search.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "search/filter by typing" — the filter must produce a consistent highlighted row between the cursor owner (App) and the renderer (TreePanel).
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx:80-91` — The test for Enter triggering drill-in moves the cursor down once (to index 1) and presses Enter, then asserts the frame contains ".aide". The test does not verify that the frame shows the DrillInPanel's card layout (scope, intent, outcomes), only that the string ".aide" appears — which is already present in tree mode. The test cannot catch the drill-in rendering breaking.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "Drilling into any .aide file opens a formatted inspection view — frontmatter rendered as a styled card"
  Misalignment: test-gap

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx` — There is no test that exercises the Enter key in drill-in mode (expand body section) or Up/Down in drill-in mode (scroll). Given issue #1 above (those keys are unimplemented), tests would fail — but their absence also means the gap went undetected.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "[enter] expand section" and "[↑↓] scroll" in drill-in mode
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Issues — Round 2 (regression from fix loop)

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.tsx:68-73` — The dir-node predicate in `visibleNodes` asks whether any file in the entire `flatNodes` array matches the search filter, not whether any child of this specific directory matches. During a search, if any single file in the project matches the filter, every directory node passes the predicate and is included in `visibleNodes`, even directories that contain no matching files. The fix for issue #4 correctly lifted `visibleNodes` to App and removed the dual-computation bug, but introduced a scoping defect in the dir predicate.
  Concretely: given a tree with `src/tools/init/intent.aide` and `src/cli/.aide`, typing "init" should show only `src/tools/init/` and its children. Instead, `src/cli/` is also shown (dir predicate matches because `intent.aide` satisfies the global `flatNodes.some` check).
  Traces to: desired outcome — "search/filter by typing" — filtered tree must contain only directories with at least one matching descendant.
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

## Issues — Round 3 (folder-first navigation implementation)

- [x] `src/cli/TreePanel/index.tsx:107-108` — The `searchFilter` guard short-circuits hint selection before `cursorOnDir` is checked. When `searchFilter` is non-empty and the cursor is on a dir node, the hint reads `[enter] drill in` — but pressing Enter will expand or collapse the dir (App.tsx line 194-215 has no special case for `searchFilter`). The hint is false. Plan step 8 requires the footer hint to always reflect cursor context: `[enter] expand` on a collapsed dir, `[enter] collapse` on an expanded dir. The `searchFilter` condition must be subordinate to `cursorOnDir`, not the other way around.
  Traces to: plan step 8 — "context-aware footer hints"; desired outcome — "keyboard navigation follows terminal conventions" (a hint that lies about what Enter does violates the convention)
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx` — No test covers the footer hint during a search when the cursor is on a dir node. The hint-text inconsistency above passed all 38 tests because no test checks what the hint says while `searchFilter` is active and `cursorOnDir` is true. A test rendering with two dirs, typing a search string that surfaces a dir, and asserting the hint contains `expand` (not `drill in`) would pin this behavior.
  Traces to: plan step 8 — "context-aware footer hints"; prior retro pattern ("a regression test pinned to the concrete behavior would have caught the defect immediately")
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Issues — Round 4 (dual-panel refactor QA)

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.tsx:263-296` — In drill-in mode, Up/Down arrow keys are consumed exclusively to move `drillFocused` (body section cursor). The tree cursor (`cursorIndex`) is not updated. The user cannot navigate to a different file while in drill-in mode without pressing Escape first. The spec strategy says "the left panel (tree) is unaffected and remains navigable" and the Good examples section explicitly shows: "While in drill-in view, the developer uses arrow keys on the left panel to highlight a different file. The right panel updates to show that file's content, staying in drill-in mode." Neither behavior is present.
  Traces to: desired outcome 3 — "The tree on the left remains visible and active throughout"; spec strategy — "the left panel (tree) is unaffected and remains navigable"
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/App/index.test.tsx` — No test navigates the tree cursor while in drill-in mode. The "tree panel remains visible during drill-in" test (line 471) asserts tree content is rendered, but it does not press Up/Down in drill-in mode and assert the tree cursor moved or that a different file's content appears in the right panel. This gap allowed the frozen-cursor behavior to pass all 43 tests undetected.
  Traces to: desired outcome 3 — "The tree on the left remains visible and active throughout"; spec strategy — "remains navigable"
  Misalignment: test-gap

## Retro

**What would have caught issue #1 (dead drill-in keyboard) earlier?**
The plan (Phase 6) explicitly specifies: "Drill-in mode: Up/Down scroll within the detail view. Enter expands/collapses the focused body section." A plan-stage checkpoint that cross-referenced the keyboard state machine against DrillInPanel's props would have caught that no dispatch path existed. The test for drill-in mode (Phase 9, App integration test) should have been written to press Enter on a collapsed section and assert the section expands — that test would have immediately exposed the missing handler.

**What would have caught issue #2 (toggle doesn't restore shallow)** earlier?
The plan says "Store both shallow and deep scan results so toggling back to shallow is instant." The implementation never stores the shallow results — it discards them when deep scan replaces `treeNodes`. A plan-stage review would have noted the missing shallow cache variable. A test that toggles deep on, then off, then asserts no summaries in the rendered tree would have caught it at the test stage.

**What would have caught issue #3 (isDeepView unused in TreePanel) earlier?**
A TypeScript lint rule for unused destructured props, or a code review pass that checked every prop in the interface is read at least once in the render body. This is a classic case of a prop that was wired in the interface but whose usage was forgotten when writing the render path.

**What would have caught issue #4 (cursor-filter desync) earlier?**
The dual computation of `visibleNodes` in two separate components is an architecture smell — the spec strategy says "all state is lifted to App" (TreePanel "manages no state"). Lifting the filter computation entirely into App and passing computed `visibleNodes` down to TreePanel (instead of re-deriving it) would have eliminated the divergence by construction. A test that typed a search string and checked the highlighted row matched a file in the filtered list would have caught this at test time.

**What would have caught the Round 2 regression (dir predicate scoping) earlier?**
A test that renders a two-directory tree, types a search term that matches only one directory's files, and asserts the other directory is NOT in the rendered output. The fix loop added no search-filter correctness tests — it only verified that `visibleNodes` was computed once. A regression test pinned to the concrete behavior would have caught the scoping defect immediately.

**What would have caught the Round 3 hint inconsistency earlier?**
Plan step 8 explicitly lists the three hint states. A reviewer cross-referencing the TreePanel hint logic against those three states would have spotted that the `searchFilter` branch pre-empts the `cursorOnDir` branch rather than composing with it. A test that types a search string and asserts the hint text based on cursor type — not just that the app renders — would have caught it. Footer hints have been a persistent source of drift across all three rounds; the pattern is that hint logic is written once and never regression-tested as navigation modes compose.

**What would have caught the Round 4 tree-navigation gap earlier?**
The spec strategy paragraph contains the phrase "remains navigable" — a QA pass that reads every sentence of the Strategy section and maps it to a concrete keyboard handler would have flagged that no Up/Down handler in drill-in mode advances `cursorIndex`. The plan does not mention tree navigation during drill-in at all — the plan scope was narrowly focused on eliminating the full-screen mode and wiring the detail panel, and the "tree stays navigable" constraint was never translated into a plan step or a test. A plan that adds a drill-in mode should always include a step: "verify that all tree-mode keyboard actions that the spec says are active during drill-in remain wired." The absence of a test for "arrow key moves tree cursor while in drill-in mode" is the detection failure — a test that drills in, presses Down, and asserts `src/tools/init/` is highlighted would have exposed it immediately.

## Issues — Round 5 (TUI enhancement QA)

- [x] `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderPlanDetail/parsePlanItems/index.ts:28` — The heading regex `/^###\s+\d+\.\s+(.+)$/` matches `### N. Title` but not the two actual plan formats used in this project. Format 1 (`src/cli/plan.aide`): `### N. [x] Step title` — the checkbox is embedded in the heading, not in a separate `- [x]` line below. The parser matches the heading (capturing `[x] Step title` as the step name) but finds zero checklist items under it, producing `0/0 complete`. Format 2 (`src/tools/init/plan.aide`, `src/tools/upgrade/plan.aide`): `### Phase N — Title` — the `Phase` word and em-dash pattern do not match `\d+\.`, so the parser sees no headings at all, groups all `- [x]` items under an empty-string step, and includes bold markers (`**item text**`) verbatim in item text. All real plan files in this project produce either `0/0 complete` or ungrouped items with markdown noise in their text.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "plan files show checklist items with completion progress"; undesired outcome — "Plan and todo files rendered through the same intent-card template... checklist items displayed as unformatted markdown body text with no completion state"
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderPlanDetail/parsePlanItems/index.test.ts:1-148` — All tests use a synthetic plan format (`### 1. Title` headings with bare `- [x] text` items below) that matches neither actual plan format in this project. The tests pass because they test the parser's own assumed format rather than the formats real plan.aide files contain. No test exercises `### Phase N — Title` headings, embedded `[x]` in headings, or bold item text (`**text**`). The test suite provides false confidence that plan files in the project will render correctly.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "plan files show checklist items with completion progress"; retro pattern — "tests that test the format the parser assumes rather than the format real files use"
  Misalignment: test-gap

- [x] `src/cli/TreePanel/index.tsx:108` and `src/util/scan/index.ts:63-88` — The TUI tree shows `file.description` only, falling back to `file.summary` (body-derived) in deep view. `aide_discover` uses `frontmatter?.description || (frontmatter?.intent ? frontmatter.intent.split(/[.\n]/)[0] : undefined)` — it derives a description from the first sentence of `intent` when `description` is absent. For the majority of specs in this project that have `intent` but no `description` field, `aide_discover` shows a summary but the TUI tree shows nothing. The spec outcome states: "The tree renders the same information an agent receives from aide_discover — descriptions, alignment status flags, file types, and ancestor chain context." The divergence breaks the debugging-surface purpose of the TUI.
  Traces to: desired outcome — "The tree renders the same information an agent receives from aide_discover"; undesired outcome — "A tree view that diverges from what aide_discover returns"
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

**What would have caught the Round 5 plan parser mismatch earlier?**
Plan step 3 says: "The plan body format is markdown with `### N. Step title` headings and `- [x]`/`- [ ]` checklist items." A researcher who read any actual plan.aide file in the project before writing this description would have seen that neither `src/cli/plan.aide` nor `src/tools/init/plan.aide` uses that format. The gap is a spec-gap: the plan step invented a canonical format instead of surveying the corpus. A QA check that runs `parsePlanItems` against at least two real plan files in the project and asserts `doneCount > 0` would have caught it immediately — the output would be `0/0` for `src/cli/plan.aide` and ungrouped bold text for the Phase-style files.

**What would have caught the Round 5 aide_discover parity gap earlier?**
Plan step 7 ("The tree renders the same information aide_discover returns") required reading `buildAncestorChain/index.ts` to understand what aide_discover actually returns. The plan did not include that read. The intent-fallback (`frontmatter.intent.split(/[.\n]/)[0]`) is a single line in `buildAncestorChain` that the implementor would have seen and replicated had they been directed to study the reference implementation before writing the tree panel logic. A plan step for parity should always cite the specific source to match.
